<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Öljeitü</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1280–<a href="/years/1316">1316</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Öljeitü was a ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a> in the early fourteenth century. He built the great domed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dome-of-soltaniyeh">mausoleum at Sultaniyya</a> around 1313, a landmark of Persian-Islamic architecture. His conversion to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/twelver-shiism">Twelver Shi'ism</a> created religious tensions within a largely <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> population but laid cultural foundations later drawn upon by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavids</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oljeitu.jpg" alt="Öljeitü" width="292" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: anonymous · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Khan_Oljaitu_accepts_Yuan_ambassadors,_as_featured_in_Majma%27_al-Tavarikh_(cropped)_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Öljaitü, also known as Mohammad-e Khodabandeh, was the eighth Ilkhanid ruler. His name means blessed in Mongolian. He was the son of the Ilkhan Arghun and his third wife, the Keraite Christian Uruk Khatun, and the brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghazan-khan">Mahmud Ghazan</a>. Born during his father's viceroyalty in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a>, he was given the name Khar-banda, meaning mule driver, raised a Buddhist and baptised in 1291 with the name Nikolya, after Pope Nicholas IV. He later converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni Islam</a> with Ghazan and took the name Muhammad. Named heir in 1299, he moved against possible rivals on hearing of Ghazan's death: his emissary killed Prince Alafrang, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaykhatu">Gaykhatu</a>, on 30 May 1304.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Öljaitü was crowned on 19 July 1304 and appointed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashid-al-din-hamadani">Rashid al-Din</a> and Sa'd al-Din Savaji as his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">viziers</a>. In 1305 he received ambassadors from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan dynasty</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chagatai-khanate">Chagatai Khanate</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>, which established a peace among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a>. He campaigned against the Kartid ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a> and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilan">Gilan</a>, where his commander-in-chief Qutluqshah was defeated and killed. Rashid al-Din completed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jami">Jami</a> al-Tawarikh on 14 April 1307. The citadel of Irbil, held by rebel Christians, fell on 1 July 1310 and its defenders were massacred. In 1312 Sa'd al-Din Savaji was arrested for corruption and executed.</p><p>His last campaign against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a> failed, though he reportedly briefly took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>. He sent embassies to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-iv-of-france">Philip IV of France</a>, Pope Clement V and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-i-of-england">Edward I of England</a> in 1305, again in 1307 and to Edward II in 1313, renewing the Mongol hope of an alliance against the Mamluks, and gave the Venetians trading rights by a treaty in 1306. Byzantine Emperor Andronicus II gave him a daughter in marriage, and in 1308 Öljaitü sent 30,000 men against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bithynia">Bithynia</a>. In 1315 his son Abu Sa'id was made governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mazandaran">Mazandaran</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Öljaitü died at Soltaniyeh in December 1316 and was succeeded by his son Abu Sa'id. Rashid al-Din Hamadani was accused of having poisoned him and was executed. He professed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> during his life. Influenced by Shi'a theologians such as Al-Hilli, and alienated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> factional strife, he changed to Shi'a Islam in 1310; a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> historian wrote that he became Sunni again in his last years. He oversaw the completion of the city of Soltaniyeh in 1306, and his tomb there, 300 km west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a>, remains the best known monument of Ilkhanid Persia.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Öljaitü” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ilkhanid culture blended Mongol, Persian and Chinese traditions, particularly in manuscript illumination and architecture, as in the great domed mausoleum at Sultaniyya built for <b>Öljeitü</b>, completed around 1313.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Amir Chupan was the most powerful commander of the Ilkhanate under <b>Öljeitü</b> and Abu Sa'id until Abu Sa'id had him killed in 1327.</blockquote><a href="/state/chupanids">Chupanids</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1306 the Ilkhan <b>Öljeitü</b> ended its rule and placed Kerman under appointed governors.</blockquote><a href="/state/qutlugh-khanid-dynasty">Qutlugh-Khanid dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultaniyya">Soltaniyeh</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=36.44,48.8&amp;year=1303" aria-label="Soltaniyeh on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5777 850 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Öljeitü"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6227 1120h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6227 1120h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=36.44,48.8&amp;year=1303">What was here in 1303? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
